hi there steve kaufman today i want to  talk about why kids learn  languages better than adults  now i know i have said in the past  that adults can learn better than kids  and this is true to the extent that  adults have more life experience have a  a larger vocabulary in their own  language  which can help them to acquire more  vocabulary in a new language so that you  know in  less than a year in czech i can read  serious books on history  in czech that a child of seven wouldn't  read  however when it comes to producing the  language understanding the language  based on  sort of the the  content based on what they're hearing  around them  uh the children do better they speak  better they speak with less of an accent  they understand better they speak more  naturally by and large they are better  in most cases now this assumes that the  child is interested  we all know of school children who for  example here in canada take french for  10 or 12 years and at the end of it they  can't speak french because they're not  interested so uh putting kids who aren't  very interested in a formal classroom is  not going to enable them to learn  however if you sent that same child who  couldn't learn in a classroom if you  sent them off to a french speaking  environment within six months they'd be  speaking fairly fluently whereas their  parents would not be  and why is that well the reason i'm sort  of making this a little video is because  i was reminded of how well kids learn  with the example of my grandson now my  grandson is very interested in playing  hockey  uh not surprising because his father  played hockey and of course i played not  very well  and he's not so interested in golf but a  friend of his  two years ago decided to uh join the  junior program at our golf club so now  after two years  this friend of my grandsons is a good  golfer shoots in the 80s has a natural  swing looks really good  my grandson hasn't really progressed in  the two years  but  i thought about the fact that at the  golf club all the kids in the junior  program they've all got these natural  swings they look good they seem to play  golf well  within a couple of years they're able to  play and then i think of the adults like  myself and others who over the last 10  years  despite  working out on the driving range and  taking lessons they essentially don't  improve  uh very much and many of them actually  go downhill  why is that why can the kids just within  a year or two just get up there  naturally they're swinging the ball and  they're mostly they're fooling around  and having fun while they're learning  and yet they seem to improve and the  adults who are so intent and they're  they read the golf magazines on what to  do with their elbow their knee their  shoulder  this and that and they don't seem to  improve very much  and  it's not it don't i mean that the kids  have fun with it they're natural they  just watch and imitate they don't doubt  themselves they just go for it they're  not resisting it  and yet the adults are the adults doubt  themselves they resist it they're trying  to fit it in with what they were doing  before they don't really accept  uh the way they're being taught to do  things  there is they don't they just don't let  themselves go  the way the kids do and i think that's  largely why kids learn languages better  than most adults again assuming that  they want that they're interested i mean  no one's going to learn if they're not  interested adult or child  uh and you know very often there is  discussion this discussion amongst  teachers you know  um  adults learn differently from children  the second or third language acquisition  is not the same as the first language  well to some extent of course this is  true if we're learning a second language  we already have a first language  uh in our system which a  provides a sort of a a filter  creates resistance to the second  language because we're kind of used to  the first language  and so we have to overcome that  resistance and second of all on the plus  side we have a lot of vocabulary in that  first language which can help us in the  second language as i said so the  circumstances are not the same  but i think the basic learning process  insofar as the brain is concerned is the  same and i heartily recommend that you  read this book by manfred spitzer that i  have referred to before  which is called learning and the brain  and i'll put a link here on  my youtube  comments section  uh but the brain needs the stimulus it  needs some models it needs a lot of  input and the brain will gradually start  to  put labels on things and create little  patterns so that it can deal with all of  this and that's basically the same so  kids do any have an easier time with  language because they're not  as hung up about hanging on to that  first language they're more prepared to  just let themselves go there they hear  something they they want to communicate  with with their peers and and they just  pick up on it they allow the brain to  just pick up on it whereas adults want  to formalize the process they want some  rules  uh  some people are into you know passing  tests  uh  they get frustrated when they don't  remember the rules or if they get a low  mark on the test kids are not into that  the kids are just oh okay that's how  they say it and gradually imitating it  so  i think that  as an adult language learner  the best thing we can do is to simplify  the process  and try to learn like kids without  doubting ourselves without feeling  frustrated just enjoying the fact that  we're  able to communicate or that we're able  to understand and exposing ourselves to  more and more  uh of the language more and more content  that we're interested in and essentially  that's what i'm doing with check i'm  reading stuff that i'm interested in i'm  listening to stuff that i'm interested  in i can't speak very well it doesn't  bother me i'm not going to write a test  i'm not going to write the european  framework i couldn't care less whether  i'm at a1 a2 b1 b2 to me that's all  meaningless as i've said we had a  discussion at our forum at lincoln on  language testing and so forth and so on  and i said i  aside from chinese where i had to write  a test for the government you know i've  never taken a test in any of these  languages and i have no interest in  doing so  i do what i enjoy doing with the  language if it's if it's reading it's  reading if it's  speaking and speaking and to the extent  that i can speak i'm happy  uh  i'm just enjoying it and just like these  kids playing golf they're just enjoying  the golf i mean yeah they are that they  would like to do well uh and but because  they're enjoying it they end up doing  well  uh whereas if you're doubting yourself  and not  you know happy and uh frustrated and  worried about your test marks and so  forth uh you want to as well in my view  so  uh i think that uh best advice for a  language learner is to uh try to learn  like kids enjoy it just let it let it  all hang out let it go in and let it  come out and don't worry about it  just like the kids  so thank you for listening bye for now
 

